Z||000023||DaRkLoRD||01-04-2001||02:13 AM||||For the record, when I was 16 I knew I wasn't responsible enough to drive a car. I waited until I turned 18 before I took the driving test. I'm a very cautious driver. the number of times I've done something really stupid, I can count on one hand probably. (of course, those are the ones everyone remembers....) And in every one of those cases, no one was hurt, and there was no permanent vehicle damage. One time I ran a car into a snowbank and we needed a realignment afterwards, but that wasn't really anything major. and it was an accident, not me fooling around.<BR>I've spun the car in a parking lot a few times, and often I'll floor it in the winter to make the tires spin and kick up some snow.. but never if there are other cars right near me or any danger of hitting anything. I do tend to drive a bit over the limit during the day.. (50 in a 40 zone, or 90-100 on the highway, limit is 80)<P>I'm very cautious at night or when there's people around though, in fact at night many people tell me to drive faster. but if I'm on the highway, and it's dark, I usually go fairly slow with my highbeams on. I've hit an animal before, and I felt pretty bad about it.. I don't want to do that again.<P>In the past 2 weeks the weather in my hometown has been especially bad for driving. 2 teenagers have died, as well as a middle-aged woman. In a town of less than 10,000 people, that's really a shock. Especially to my sister, who was good friends with one of the guys who died, and is friends with the girlfriend of the 2nd guy.<BR>I didn't know any of the people that died in these accidents, yet I was still saddened by the news. I was out driving on the same highway as the 2nd guy, at the same time. On my way back from picking up my sister at a friends house, I could see the flashing lights further down the road so I took an alternate route home.. Every time I think it might be fun to do something REALLY crazy, or see how fast I can take a turn without the back end breaking loose, I think of how my family would feel if I died, or how I'd feel if I caused someone else to die.. and most of the time.. I slow down immediatly. The other times, it's usually because it's not so crazy and I know I'll be fine.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>(and so far, I have been.. I keep wondering when the urge to floor it and whip around curves will go away so I can drive responsibly 100% of the time.. hehe)<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||9
Z||000024||RaeQwan||01-04-2001||02:26 AM||||Ever since a friend of mine from school(one of the few) and my nephews mother were killed in two differant wrecks.. I've always felt saddend anytime I hear about anything like it. But in respect to the Fiero, I say this yet again shows the true safety factor these cars have.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/raeqwanegg.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000025||Tigger||01-04-2001||07:21 AM||||WoW!<P>Good to know the guy in the Fiero is in stable condition and should be ok!  looking at what's left of his Fiero...  sheesh.<P>Sad to hear the guy in the Subaru was killed.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>||||reg||11
Z||000026||Raydar||01-04-2001||10:07 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroLisa:<BR><B>Racer1: Age isn't everything. I think I'm an extremely responsible (18 yr old) driver, as I'm sure several of the younger people on this forum are. I have never caused an accident and in all reality I rarely *really* speed (5-10 over not included)<BR>Everyone of any age can do something stupid and cause an accident such as this.<BR>Sometimes, driving the Fiero, I get downright scared of idiot drivers.<BR>I still can't believe that Fiero owner survived. <P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>&lt;putting on flame suit&gt;<BR>I also believe that testosterone has a lot to do with it. (In other words, raising hell is more of a "guy" thing.) That's why young male drivers have to pay more for insurance than the ladies. <P>Having said all of that, I believe that raising the driving age may just result in older inexperienced drivers. I believe that what we really need are driver training programs, and much more difficult exams.<BR>As it is now, you can get a GA driver's license if you can pass a "multiple guess" test, and drive around a parking lot without killing any orange cones. Sad.<BR> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000027||TheFiero64||01-04-2001||10:28 AM||||We should all get together and have a card made to give to the Fiero driver. If we could locate where to send it.||||reg||1
Z||000028||stsmithgt||01-04-2001||10:41 AM||||My parents were extremely strict with driver privileges so the first year I dotted my I's and crossed my T's.  The second year was where the dare devil driving started, but by that time I was aware that off street hot rodding like puddle jumping down mud roads by the tomatoe and corn fields. It was a lot more fun than getting tickets messing around on the street. We had strict police too.  Too easy to get tickets so I found out.  It sounds like the parents of that kid tried to train him.  I don't think they should have let him drive such an authoritative vehicle like an SUV (by himself).  If they can afford an SUV, they could have picked up a more neutral car to be his first.  My folks told me no one will live in their house and own a "Hot Rod". Kids do get power hungry with all that testosterone flowing.  ||||reg||11
